and then I can't remember what they just said.

2531
01:21:27.064 --> 01:21:28.237
It happens to everyone, it happens to me too.

2532
01:21:28.237 --> 01:21:29.901
It's terrible.
It's such a bad feeling.

2533
01:21:29.901 --> 01:21:31.610
So I was like, I can't allow that to happen.

2534
01:21:31.610 --> 01:21:35.360
So just intuitively, I was like starting to do that,

2535
01:21:35.360 --> 01:21:37.390
just so I could be in that head space.

2536
01:21:37.390 --> 01:21:39.300
Yeah.
But it's tricky,

2537
01:21:39.300 --> 01:21:42.785
'cause if you start saying the name of the person too much,

2538
01:21:42.785 --> 01:21:45.390
then it's not good.
It's awkward, yes.

2539
01:21:45.390 --> 01:21:47.223
Then you really sound salesy.

2540
01:21:47.223 --> 01:21:49.500
I think that listening is one of the big elements

2541
01:21:49.500 --> 01:21:51.630
that can help people, just a very quick, easy one.

2542
01:21:51.630 --> 01:21:53.150
It's a muscle, it's like anything else

2543
01:21:53.150 --> 01:21:54.650
that you become a better listener,

2544
01:21:54.650 --> 01:21:56.220
I think you become better as a parent,

2545
01:21:56.220 --> 01:21:57.660
as a spouse, as a sibling.

2546
01:21:57.660 --> 01:22:00.350
Like most people don't really practice it,

2547
01:22:00.350 --> 01:22:01.183
because what you're doing

2548
01:22:01.183 --> 01:22:03.599
is trying to wait for your turn to speak.

2549
01:22:03.599 --> 01:22:04.893
Right?
Right.

2550
01:22:04.893 --> 01:22:05.757
It's almost like the Dale Carnegie,

2551
01:22:05.757 --> 01:22:07.361
"How To Win Friends and Influence People".

2552
01:22:07.361 --> 01:22:09.360
I'll go back and read that book again, and it's from,

2553
01:22:09.360 --> 01:22:11.310
I don't know, almost 100 years ago now,

2554
01:22:11.310 --> 01:22:12.867
I don't know the publication date.

2555
01:22:12.867 --> 01:22:15.380
But I find gems in there that are so obvious,

2556
01:22:15.380 --> 01:22:16.730
over and over, as to,

2557
01:22:16.730 --> 01:22:19.934
it's what you said earlier, benefits-oriented.

2558
01:22:19.934 --> 01:22:22.530
It's better, in a podcast situation it's difficult,

2559
01:22:22.530 --> 01:22:23.497
'cause I'm here to talk about myself,

2560
01:22:23.497 --> 01:22:24.557
but I try to deflect when people ask me

2561
01:22:24.557 --> 01:22:26.280
about what I do all the time,

2562
01:22:26.280 --> 01:22:27.940
and learn more about what they do,

2563
01:22:27.940 --> 01:22:29.630
and see what what's intriguing about them.

2564
01:22:29.630 --> 01:22:32.890
What's the topic that's most vital to them?

2565
01:22:32.890 --> 01:22:34.620
Is it their family, is it their career?

2566
01:22:34.620 --> 01:22:36.040
And the more I learn about them,

2567
01:22:36.040 --> 01:22:36.873
the more they open up to me,

2568
01:22:36.873 --> 01:22:38.700
and the more comfortable they are around me.

2569
01:22:38.700 --> 01:22:40.170
And that's kind of like when I do the cocktail hour,

2570
01:22:40.170 --> 01:22:41.760
when you said, when I'm at a theater and I'm walking around,

2571
01:22:41.760 --> 01:22:43.310
I'm saying hello, I don't want someone to be starstruck.

2572
01:22:43.310 --> 01:22:44.167
I go,

2573
01:22:44.167 --> 01:22:46.790
"Oh my God, you saw that TV show, what did you think?"

2574
01:22:46.790 --> 01:22:47.623
I go,

2575
01:22:47.623 --> 01:22:49.680
"Tell me this, what would you do if you were on there?

2576
01:22:49.680 --> 01:22:50.513
What did you think-"

2577
01:22:50.513 --> 01:22:52.460
I'll just find some way to ask a question

2578
01:22:52.460 --> 01:22:54.810
that deflects from talking about me,

2579
01:22:54.810 --> 01:22:57.560
and to get a little more of them, and try to open them up

2580
01:22:57.560 --> 01:22:59.800
because that's the best way to kind of cut the ice,

2581
01:22:59.800 --> 01:23:01.270
cut the tension, and that's in a setting

2582
01:23:01.270 --> 01:23:02.890
where I'm trying to read their mind later,

2583
01:23:02.890 --> 01:23:04.820
I don't want them to be on guard,

2584
01:23:04.820 --> 01:23:06.273
I wanna find things that are interesting to them.

2585
01:23:06.273 --> 01:23:08.470
Like if I ask you about a memory of your own,

2586
01:23:08.470 --> 01:23:10.810
like doing a race, that you dedicated

2587
01:23:10.810 --> 01:23:12.920
your life for two years to get to that point,

2588
01:23:12.920 --> 01:23:14.350
you'll get focused on yourself,

2589
01:23:14.350 --> 01:23:16.750
you start forgetting that I'm trying to watch you,

2590
01:23:16.750 --> 01:23:17.764
and look at what you're doing.

2591
01:23:17.764 --> 01:23:18.597
That's it.

2592
01:23:18.597 --> 01:23:20.570
You asked me earlier, how do I make you relax?

2593
01:23:20.570 --> 01:23:21.653
Well, I found a way.

2594
01:23:23.689 --> 01:23:25.250
Let's talk about running.

2595
01:23:25.250 --> 01:23:26.780
Yeah.
So when does

2596
01:23:26.780 --> 01:23:30.570
the running thing start to work its way into your life?

2597
01:23:30.570 --> 01:23:32.480
So I have twin older sisters,

2598
01:23:32.480 --> 01:23:33.900
they're about 7 1/2 years older.

2599
01:23:33.900 --> 01:23:35.400
And there was always a little bit of...

2600
01:23:35.400 --> 01:23:36.834
'Cause I was a baby brother,

2601
01:23:36.834 --> 01:23:38.300
I don't wanna say sibling rivalry,

2602
01:23:38.300 --> 01:23:39.337
but I always wanted to kind of have some sort

2603
01:23:39.337 --> 01:23:42.190
of one upsmanship on my older sisters.

2604
01:23:42.190 --> 01:23:46.340
And in 2004, I'm working like a day job,

2605
01:23:46.340 --> 01:23:47.910
I think a lot of people fall into this trap

2606
01:23:47.910 --> 01:23:50.650
where really you start going out to happy hours,

2607
01:23:50.650 --> 01:23:52.700
you're eating like crap, you don't feel good.

2608
01:23:52.700 --> 01:23:53.810
Like, I didn't really feel as good,

2609
01:23:53.810 --> 01:23:55.050
I was letting myself go in away,

2610
01:23:55.050 --> 01:23:58.840
and just unhealthy lifestyle choices.

2611
01:23:58.840 --> 01:24:01.336
And I was missing a goal.

2612
01:24:01.336 --> 01:24:03.340
Listen, this is a very comfortable thing to say,

2613
01:24:03.340 --> 01:24:04.750
but I found life boring in a way,

2614
01:24:04.750 --> 01:24:06.790
because it became very repetitive.

2615
01:24:06.790 --> 01:24:08.920
And listen, these are very first world problems,

2616
01:24:08.920 --> 01:24:09.860
there's people that don't have food,

2617
01:24:09.860 --> 01:24:10.990
that don't know how to pay their next bill,

2618
01:24:10.990 --> 01:24:13.540
I didn't have that issue, but I wanted a challenge,

2619
01:24:13.540 --> 01:24:14.420
and I think a lot of people

2620
01:24:14.420 --> 01:24:15.740
are drawn towards endurance sports,

2621
01:24:15.740 --> 01:24:19.280
because there is no challenge, it's something is lacking.

2622
01:24:19.280 --> 01:24:21.270
I want that illusion of danger,

2623
01:24:21.270 --> 01:24:22.640
whatever you wanna call it, challenging myself,

2624
01:24:22.640 --> 01:24:24.360
seeing what my medal is.

2625
01:24:24.360 --> 01:24:25.193
So my sister signed up for a marathon,

2626
01:24:25.193 --> 01:24:27.180
I thought this was insane,

2627
01:24:27.180 --> 01:24:29.780
because in 2004, it wasn't mainstream,

2628
01:24:29.780 --> 01:24:30.854
not everyone was doing marathons.

2629
01:24:30.854 --> 01:24:32.511
So I on a whim signed up for a marathon.

2630
01:24:32.511 --> 01:24:33.344
Somebody tells me,

2631
01:24:33.344 --> 01:24:34.177
"You should do the Boston marathon."

2632
01:24:34.177 --> 01:24:35.010
I go,

2633
01:24:35.010 --> 01:24:35.843
"Okay, how do I do that?"

2634
01:24:35.843 --> 01:24:36.676
And they're like,

2635
01:24:36.676 --> 01:24:37.509
"Well, you have to qualify."

2636
01:24:37.509 --> 01:24:38.347
I'm like,

2637
01:24:38.347 --> 01:24:39.180
"Well, okay, how do I-"

2638
01:24:39.180 --> 01:24:40.550
"So you have to run one really fast."

2639
01:24:40.550 --> 01:24:42.100
So I reverse engineered.

2640
01:24:42.100 --> 01:24:43.727
I found out what the Boston Marathon time is, and I go,

2641
01:24:43.727 --> 01:24:44.880
"I'm gonna run that."

2642
01:24:44.880 --> 01:24:47.670
Not knowing that you have to kind of be fast to do that.

2643
01:24:47.670 --> 01:24:49.160
So then I started running on a treadmill,

2644
01:24:49.160 --> 01:24:52.140
didn't read any books about how should you train.

2645
01:24:52.140 --> 01:24:54.920
And I did a calculator. I probably did in my head.

2646
01:24:54.920 --> 01:24:56.660
I'm like, this is how many miles per hour,

2647
01:24:56.660 --> 01:24:58.350
so I just set the treadmill-

2648
01:24:58.350 --> 01:24:59.880
I'm just gonna run that pace-

2649
01:24:59.880 --> 01:25:00.960
All the time.
Every run.

2650
01:25:00.960 --> 01:25:03.481
Yeah, and so that didn't work well,

2651
01:25:03.481 --> 01:25:05.280
'cause that was a little too fast.

2652
01:25:05.280 --> 01:25:06.927
And later on, I learned what heart rate training was,

2653
01:25:06.927 --> 01:25:08.160
and what have you.

2654
01:25:08.160 --> 01:25:11.460
So I do the first marathon, I have some fitness in me,

2655
01:25:11.460 --> 01:25:14.210
I end up running that pace for about 17 miles,

2656
01:25:14.210 --> 01:25:15.260
I did the Philadelphia Marathon,

2657
01:25:15.260 --> 01:25:17.685
and then I just fall apart, like traditional-

2658
01:25:17.685 --> 01:25:19.177
It's the classic story.

2659
01:25:19.177 --> 01:25:21.310
17 miles, 17, 18.
Dude, (hand slaps).

2660
01:25:21.310 --> 01:25:22.690
You hear that noise?

2661
01:25:22.690 --> 01:25:23.730
That's me hitting the wall.

2662
01:25:23.730 --> 01:25:27.230
I was like crying pretty much at mile 21.

2663
01:25:27.230 --> 01:25:31.230
And at one point, I'm just walking, and I'm so broken.

2664
01:25:31.230 --> 01:25:33.370
And I see these two guys walking in front of me,

2665
01:25:33.370 --> 01:25:35.120
everyone else is running, 'cause I was going pretty fast,

2666
01:25:35.120 --> 01:25:35.953
so at that point, you know,

2667
01:25:35.953 --> 01:25:37.430
it's like "The Walking Dead" where I am.

2668
01:25:37.430 --> 01:25:39.120
Little did I know that once you start doing ultras,

2669
01:25:39.120 --> 01:25:40.950
that's the real "Walking dead".

2670
01:25:40.950 --> 01:25:42.000
But I see these two guys walking,

2671
01:25:42.000 --> 01:25:45.057
that are about 200, 300 yards ahead of me, really far.

2672
01:25:45.057 --> 01:25:46.660
And so I get enough energy to be like,

2673
01:25:46.660 --> 01:25:47.677
I'm gonna run to them,

2674
01:25:47.677 --> 01:25:50.600
and I'll walk the rest of the run with them, I'll walk.

2675
01:25:50.600 --> 01:25:51.970
So misery loves company.

2676
01:25:51.970 --> 01:25:53.800
So I run for like 30 seconds,

2677
01:25:53.800 --> 01:25:56.350
and I'm getting within the point where I can hear them,

2678
01:25:56.350 --> 01:25:58.010
and I see these two guys,

2679
01:25:58.010 --> 01:25:59.530
I don't know who they are to this day,

2680
01:25:59.530 --> 01:26:01.260
but I hate them and I love them.

2681
01:26:01.260 --> 01:26:02.610
They looked at each other, they nodded,

2682
01:26:02.610 --> 01:26:03.740
and they started running,

2683
01:26:03.740 --> 01:26:05.537
and I literally screamed on,

2684
01:26:05.537 --> 01:26:06.370
"F you!"

2685
01:26:06.370 --> 01:26:08.600
'Cause I was so mad, why did you start running?

2686
01:26:08.600 --> 01:26:09.920
And I got this adrenaline,

2687
01:26:09.920 --> 01:26:12.150
that I kept running from that point on.

2688
01:26:12.150 --> 01:26:14.570
And I actually ran the rest of the race, slow mind you.

2689
01:26:14.570 --> 01:26:16.629
If somebody could fact check it,

2690
01:26:16.629 --> 01:26:19.038
I don't know, I ran like 320 something, 325,

2691
01:26:19.038 --> 01:26:20.820
320 something, but I was hooked.

2692
01:26:20.820 --> 01:26:23.130
My sister did one marathon, was like never a again,

2693
01:26:23.130 --> 01:26:24.577
I did that one, and I was like,

2694
01:26:24.577 --> 01:26:27.160
"I'm gonna read how to train, I love this."

2695
01:26:27.160 --> 01:26:28.197
And then I started getting into it,

2696
01:26:28.197 --> 01:26:30.110
and I started chopping time,

2697
01:26:30.110 --> 01:26:32.350
and getting faster and faster and faster.

2698
01:26:32.350 --> 01:26:34.019
I did Ironmans.
Yeah.

2699
01:26:34.019 --> 01:26:35.827
Because, again, I feel like you

2700
01:26:35.827 --> 01:26:38.337
have some nut case friend who's into this,

2701
01:26:38.337 --> 01:26:40.972
I had a buddy at the time, Michael Arnstein-

2702
01:26:40.972 --> 01:26:42.050
I know Michael.
The fruitarian.

2703
01:26:42.050 --> 01:26:43.660
Yeah, I've had him on the show,

2704
01:26:43.660 --> 01:26:45.130
early, early days.
Early days.

2705
01:26:45.130 --> 01:26:45.963
So I saw Michael-

2706
01:26:45.963 --> 01:26:47.250
Back in his office in New York.

2707
01:26:47.250 --> 01:26:49.394
Yeah, I know that office well,

2708
01:26:49.394 --> 01:26:50.880
I had my 30th birthday party there.

2709
01:26:50.880 --> 01:26:53.170
And so I met him, we both read books,

2710
01:26:53.170 --> 01:26:56.180
Dean (indistinct) was like huge inspiration,

2711
01:26:56.180 --> 01:26:58.070
(indistinct), like Scott (indistinct).

2712
01:26:58.070 --> 01:26:58.963
I started reading these books,

2713
01:26:58.963 --> 01:27:01.659
and it was like a gateway drug where, we did a marathon,

2714
01:27:01.659 --> 01:27:04.030
we each signed up for a 50 miler together.

2715
01:27:04.030 --> 01:27:05.887
We were supposed to do the 50 mile together the first time,

2716
01:27:05.887 --> 01:27:08.427
and at mile three he dropped me and ran ahead, I was like,

2717
01:27:08.427 --> 01:27:09.260
"Judas!"

2718
01:27:09.260 --> 01:27:10.713
I end up passing him later.

2719
01:27:11.600 --> 01:27:13.150
Then you read about Western States,

2720
01:27:13.150 --> 01:27:14.840
so I'm like, I gotta do Western States,

2721
01:27:14.840 --> 01:27:17.220
and then Leadville, and then I end up doing Badwater,

2722
01:27:17.220 --> 01:27:18.900
and just all of these...

2723
01:27:18.900 --> 01:27:19.740
It's a whirlwind.

2724
01:27:19.740 --> 01:27:21.700
Every single time before I did a lot of these races,

2725
01:27:21.700 --> 01:27:22.560
I met somebody who did Badwater,

2726
01:27:22.560 --> 01:27:23.800
I thought they were insane.

2727
01:27:23.800 --> 01:27:25.627
But then suddenly you meet them, and you're like,

2728
01:27:25.627 --> 01:27:28.400
"I guess it's not that insane, that person did it,

2729
01:27:28.400 --> 01:27:29.410
I could probably do it."

2730
01:27:29.410 --> 01:27:32.770
And so everything became possible at a certain point,

2731
01:27:32.770 --> 01:27:34.120
because you realize it isn't impossible,

2732
01:27:34.120 --> 01:27:35.870
other people have done it.

2733
01:27:35.870 --> 01:27:37.650
And I guess that was the challenge.

2734
01:27:37.650 --> 01:27:38.483
Yeah, that's wild.

2735
01:27:38.483 --> 01:27:41.600
So you've done Badwater, Western States, Spartathlon-

2736
01:27:41.600 --> 01:27:43.150
Spartathlon was amazing.

2737
01:27:43.150 --> 01:27:45.613
If anybody hears this, and is into running,

2738
01:27:45.613 --> 01:27:47.850
and wants something, man, Spartathlon is just epic.

2739
01:27:47.850 --> 01:27:49.330
Did you do that with Arnstein?

2740
01:27:49.330 --> 01:27:50.700
We did it twice, it's on YouTube,

2741
01:27:50.700 --> 01:27:52.213
the videos are incredible, yeah.

2742
01:27:52.213 --> 01:27:53.880
How's Michael doing?
I think he's doing well.

2743
01:27:53.880 --> 01:27:55.455
Is he doing good?
Yeah.

2744
01:27:55.455 --> 01:27:57.830
I know that he...

2745
01:27:57.830 --> 01:27:59.610
I mean, I haven't talked to him in many years,

2746
01:27:59.610 --> 01:28:03.510
but I know that he would run..

2747
01:28:03.510 --> 01:28:05.230
He lived like in the suburbs-

2748
01:28:05.230 --> 01:28:06.500
Yeah, he'd run 15 miles

2749
01:28:06.500 --> 01:28:07.333
to and from his office.

2750
01:28:07.333 --> 01:28:08.840
Yeah, he'd run to and from his office,

2751
01:28:08.840 --> 01:28:10.100
which was like in Midtown.

2752
01:28:10.100 --> 01:28:11.300
It was 30 miles a day.
And he would

2753
01:28:11.300 --> 01:28:13.680
just eat fruit, off the fruit cars fruitarian, right?

2754
01:28:13.680 --> 01:28:15.040
Did he get you on the fruitarian diet?

2755
01:28:15.040 --> 01:28:16.190
I was doing fruitarian for a while.

2756
01:28:16.190 --> 01:28:17.774
I was never full fruitarian,

2757
01:28:17.774 --> 01:28:18.960
but what I would do is I'd eat fruit,

2758
01:28:18.960 --> 01:28:19.857
it's almost like a Jesse Itzler thing.

2759
01:28:19.857 --> 01:28:22.140
I'd eat fruit till 5:00 PM,

2760
01:28:22.140 --> 01:28:24.982
and my body got adjusted to that really well,

2761
01:28:24.982 --> 01:28:26.840
and then I'd eat pretty health at night,

2762
01:28:26.840 --> 01:28:28.383
and I still do a very similar thing

2763
01:28:28.383 --> 01:28:30.220
when I cut weight for marathons,

2764
01:28:30.220 --> 01:28:32.940
I'm not heavy by any stretch, but like for New York City,

2765
01:28:32.940 --> 01:28:34.710
I cut about 12 pounds.

2766
01:28:34.710 --> 01:28:36.100
Because when you're trying to run fast,

2767
01:28:36.100 --> 01:28:37.341
like ultras are one thing,

2768
01:28:37.341 --> 01:28:39.320
but when you're trying to run fast, you gotta be lean.

2769
01:28:39.320 --> 01:28:40.580
Yeah.
Like my wife

2770
01:28:40.580 --> 01:28:42.173
does not like it, she calls it manorexic,

2771
01:28:42.173 --> 01:28:44.440
but I will be pounding bananas all morning.

2772
01:28:44.440 --> 01:28:45.570
I'll be mangoes, I mean,

2773
01:28:45.570 --> 01:28:47.810
I can eat yellow til I'm yellow in the face.

2774
01:28:47.810 --> 01:28:48.700
That's not necessarily

2775
01:28:48.700 --> 01:28:50.648
the best strategy with ultras though-

2776
01:28:50.648 --> 01:28:52.530
No, then keep in mind,

2777
01:28:52.530 --> 01:28:54.170
I eat other things at night to fill out,

2778
01:28:54.170 --> 01:28:57.520
and like lean proteins, I love rice,

2779
01:28:57.520 --> 01:28:59.400
literally rice is my favorite food.

2780
01:28:59.400 --> 01:29:00.270
I could give up anything,

2781
01:29:00.270 --> 01:29:02.810
but I just love rice, and spicy hot sauce,

2782
01:29:02.810 --> 01:29:04.900
which is kind of gross, it sounds like, but just-

2783
01:29:04.900 --> 01:29:07.440
And so I will lose weight,

2784
01:29:07.440 --> 01:29:08.840
and I can lose like a pound a week,

2785
01:29:08.840 --> 01:29:11.460
and it feels amazing when you're running fast.

2786
01:29:11.460 --> 01:29:12.810
What's your favorite race,

2787
01:29:12.810 --> 01:29:14.380
or the hardest race that you've done?

2788
01:29:14.380 --> 01:29:16.837
I think Spartathlon, because it's so epic-

2789
01:29:16.837 --> 01:29:19.263
(indistinct).
Yeah, it's so epic.

2790
01:29:21.360 --> 01:29:22.820
I've never been at a race where every...

2791
01:29:22.820 --> 01:29:25.260
It feels like the Olympics for the modern man.

2792
01:29:25.260 --> 01:29:27.969
Ironman, Hawaii is cool, but it has less of a soul,

2793
01:29:27.969 --> 01:29:29.800
it feels like a corporate thing,

2794
01:29:29.800 --> 01:29:30.950
I'm not trying to knock it.

2795
01:29:30.950 --> 01:29:33.490
It's amazing, the history of it, and I'm around just,

2796
01:29:33.490 --> 01:29:34.620
everybody's a badass.

2797
01:29:34.620 --> 01:29:37.103
Like when you go to Hawaii, I just see somebody

2798
01:29:37.103 --> 01:29:37.936
and I'm like,

2799
01:29:37.936 --> 01:29:40.710
"Oh, I meet this woman who looks like she's 32, she's 62,

2800
01:29:40.710 --> 01:29:42.020
and she's gonna to completely crush me at the race too."

2801
01:29:42.020 --> 01:29:42.853
You know what I mean?

2802
01:29:42.853 --> 01:29:44.200
Like everybody there,

2803
01:29:44.200 --> 01:29:46.770
is the top of the top at everything too.

2804
01:29:46.770 --> 01:29:48.490
They're all people that are so accomplished in life,

2805
01:29:48.490 --> 01:29:50.430
you meet them, and like you run a business,

2806
01:29:50.430 --> 01:29:51.420
and you're a mother of four,

2807
01:29:51.420 --> 01:29:53.087
and you're like the best...

2808
01:29:53.087 --> 01:29:55.490
I was blown away by the people I met.

2809
01:29:55.490 --> 01:29:58.810
But Spartathlon, I met people that are...

2810
01:29:58.810 --> 01:30:01.280
Like, when I did it the first time at mile 76,

2811
01:30:01.280 --> 01:30:03.940
I DNF'd, I was not mentally prepared

2812
01:30:03.940 --> 01:30:05.850
for running 153 miles in a row,

2813
01:30:05.850 --> 01:30:07.560
I just didn't know what that was.

2814
01:30:07.560 --> 01:30:09.270
I thought I did, but I didn't.

2815
01:30:09.270 --> 01:30:11.580
And I got passed by these two guys,

2816
01:30:11.580 --> 01:30:12.950
that are in their 50s that are German,

2817
01:30:12.950 --> 01:30:14.280
and I'll never forget this image.

2818
01:30:14.280 --> 01:30:16.000
I'm on the floor, I'm done.

2819
01:30:16.000 --> 01:30:17.820
Like turn me over, I'm cooked.

2820
01:30:17.820 --> 01:30:20.040
I'm waiting to write my DNF speech.

2821
01:30:20.040 --> 01:30:21.440
Keep in mind I've been throwing up for eight hours,

2822
01:30:21.440 --> 01:30:22.690
that's a different story you can find on YouTube,

2823
01:30:22.690 --> 01:30:26.380
it's hilarious, but still, excuses, I should have finished.

2824
01:30:26.380 --> 01:30:28.920
These guys are running, in the middle of the night,

2825
01:30:28.920 --> 01:30:31.180
in Greece, drinking beers, these two German guys,

2826
01:30:31.180 --> 01:30:33.750
drinking beers, I'm not joking, they have beers,

2827
01:30:33.750 --> 01:30:36.400
they look like they're out for a jog at mile two.

2828
01:30:36.400 --> 01:30:37.490
Their mental toughness

2829
01:30:37.490 --> 01:30:40.980
is they will die before they don't finish this race.

2830
01:30:40.980 --> 01:30:44.205
And I came home, and I was so embarrassed,

2831
01:30:44.205 --> 01:30:45.088
do you understand you?

2832
01:30:45.088 --> 01:30:47.670
I watched the people at the finish line, I was crying,

2833
01:30:47.670 --> 01:30:49.370
I was so emotional, and you could see in this video.

2834
01:30:49.370 --> 01:30:50.240
'Cause I'm seeing people

2835
01:30:50.240 --> 01:30:54.040
that are way less fit than I am in every objective measure.

2836
01:30:54.040 --> 01:30:56.490
I'm like a 2/25 marathoner at the time,

2837
01:30:56.490 --> 01:30:57.660
I'm seeing a woman in her 60s,

2838
01:30:57.660 --> 01:31:00.210
who can't run a 4 1/2 marathon to save her life,

2839
01:31:00.210 --> 01:31:02.600
who finished this race when I gave up.

2840
01:31:02.600 --> 01:31:04.680
And it's so inspiring, its so humbling,

2841
01:31:04.680 --> 01:31:06.620
that when I came back the next year, it was like,

2842
01:31:06.620 --> 01:31:09.080
my mindset was, there is no way I'm not finishing.

2843
01:31:09.080 --> 01:31:10.230
Right.
And when we finished,

2844
01:31:10.230 --> 01:31:11.810
I like broke down, I'm on my knees.

2845
01:31:11.810 --> 01:31:16.227
And it's also epic because the Greek community-

2846
01:31:17.428 --> 01:31:20.360
Explain the idea behind the race,

2847
01:31:20.360 --> 01:31:21.193
for people that don't know.

2848
01:31:21.193 --> 01:31:22.900
So if anyone saw the movie "300",

2849
01:31:22.900 --> 01:31:24.070
that Gerald Butler flick,

2850
01:31:24.070 --> 01:31:25.970
they have a scene in it, where they say,

2851
01:31:25.970 --> 01:31:27.050
the way they frame it is that,

2852
01:31:27.050 --> 01:31:29.630
the Persians were gonna invade Greece,

2853
01:31:29.630 --> 01:31:32.640
and that in Athens, they sent (indistinct)

2854
01:31:32.640 --> 01:31:34.380
from, how am I blanking?

2855
01:31:34.380 --> 01:31:35.890
The Acropolis.

2856
01:31:35.890 --> 01:31:36.750
They sent this guy running,

2857
01:31:36.750 --> 01:31:38.840
and they retraced the steps 153 miles,

2858
01:31:38.840 --> 01:31:41.290
they said it was in 36 hours, this guy's in flip flops.

2859
01:31:41.290 --> 01:31:44.430
He ran there and delivered a message to king Leonidus,

2860
01:31:44.430 --> 01:31:47.740
of the Spartans, and 300 Spartans at...

2861
01:31:47.740 --> 01:31:48.847
Forgive me if I forget the place-

2862
01:31:48.847 --> 01:31:50.580
Thermopylae?
Thermopylae, yeah.

2863
01:31:50.580 --> 01:31:54.250
Right there, near Corinth that they saved,

2864
01:31:54.250 --> 01:31:57.420
they held off the Persians thousands,

2865
01:31:57.420 --> 01:31:58.490
tens of thousand of them,

2866
01:31:58.490 --> 01:32:00.060
and that gave enough time for the Greeks

2867
01:32:00.060 --> 01:32:02.940
to assemble an army, and it eventually saved civilization.

2868
01:32:02.940 --> 01:32:04.160
Like they frame it as we would not

2869
01:32:04.160 --> 01:32:06.610
have democracy here and now, if they hadn't done this,

2870
01:32:06.610 --> 01:32:09.140
and if this one runner hadn't done this epic run.

2871
01:32:09.140 --> 01:32:12.810
And so when you get there, everybody's honking their horns,

2872
01:32:12.810 --> 01:32:14.300
everybody knows, everyone's behind it.

2873
01:32:14.300 --> 01:32:16.950
When you get in town, they have like a parade

2874
01:32:16.950 --> 01:32:18.920
with thousands of people from the city,

2875
01:32:18.920 --> 01:32:21.040
they put a crown on your head

2876
01:32:21.040 --> 01:32:23.880
of olive branches that are wrapped together,

2877
01:32:23.880 --> 01:32:25.810
and you drink water that people give you in (indistinct).

2878
01:32:25.810 --> 01:32:26.643
When you get within a mile,

2879
01:32:26.643 --> 01:32:29.510
all these kids in bikes are there, everyone's honking,

2880
01:32:29.510 --> 01:32:32.020
the whole city knows what you're doing.

2881
01:32:32.020 --> 01:32:33.720
It's not like a Boston Marathon, it's not anything,

2882
01:32:33.720 --> 01:32:35.190
this is in their soul.

2883
01:32:35.190 --> 01:32:37.360
These people are so proud, I can't even tell you,

2884
01:32:37.360 --> 01:32:39.890
you bow before this statue, and deliver a message,

2885
01:32:39.890 --> 01:32:41.110
and you like hand it.

2886
01:32:41.110 --> 01:32:43.020
And it's like a religious spiritual experience.

2887
01:32:43.020 --> 01:32:44.320
There's only like, I don't know,

2888
01:32:44.320 --> 01:32:46.096
60 finishers or something like that?

2889
01:32:46.096 --> 01:32:47.846
Every year is like 20%.
Right?

2890
01:32:49.050 --> 01:32:51.270
And the year that we finished, up until that time,

2891
01:32:51.270 --> 01:32:52.517
was the lowest finisher rate ever,

2892
01:32:52.517 --> 01:32:54.203
'cause it was 95 degrees, there's no ice.

2893
01:32:54.203 --> 01:32:56.180
It's like Europe, you know where there's no ice?

2894
01:32:56.180 --> 01:32:57.490
There's no ice.

2895
01:32:57.490 --> 01:32:59.270
There's a funny story with Scott Jurich,

2896
01:32:59.270 --> 01:33:00.670
have you ever had him on?

2897
01:33:00.670 --> 01:33:02.300
No, but I know him.
I believe he's vegan.

2898
01:33:02.300 --> 01:33:04.110
He couldn't get ice in his race report anywhere,

2899
01:33:04.110 --> 01:33:05.750
the only place I think his girlfriend

2900
01:33:05.750 --> 01:33:06.670
or wife at the time could get ice,

2901
01:33:06.670 --> 01:33:07.900
I don't wanna spoil this story, or like,

2902
01:33:07.900 --> 01:33:10.100
massacre it, I remember is at a butcher shop,

2903
01:33:10.100 --> 01:33:11.307
and he had to put ice all over

2904
01:33:11.307 --> 01:33:13.240
him that was filled with blood, and this guy's a vegan,

2905
01:33:13.240 --> 01:33:15.500
with animal blood all over his body.

2906
01:33:15.500 --> 01:33:16.600
I hadn't heard that story.

2907
01:33:16.600 --> 01:33:19.130
Great story, so-
Didn't Dean run it

2908
01:33:19.130 --> 01:33:20.020
in the sandals?

2909
01:33:20.020 --> 01:33:21.860
Yeah, Dean ran it in sandals,

2910
01:33:21.860 --> 01:33:22.760
I can't remember how he did,

2911
01:33:22.760 --> 01:33:25.150
I love Dean so much, man, the greatest.

2912
01:33:25.150 --> 01:33:27.260
Dean got me into ultra running,

2913
01:33:27.260 --> 01:33:28.720
I have his book, he autographed it,

2914
01:33:28.720 --> 01:33:30.810
and then we became friends since,

2915
01:33:30.810 --> 01:33:32.850
but I have things like highlighted,

2916
01:33:32.850 --> 01:33:36.930
and I made him initial the line where he ran from Napa,

2917
01:33:36.930 --> 01:33:39.680
to San Francisco for 100 miles overnight,

2918
01:33:39.680 --> 01:33:40.677
to then run the marathon, I'm like,

2919
01:33:40.677 --> 01:33:42.250
"Dude, you are just-"

2920
01:33:42.250 --> 01:33:44.023
I love Dean, man.

2921
01:33:44.023 --> 01:33:46.400
So what is the overlap?

2922
01:33:46.400 --> 01:33:49.765
Like how do these two worlds kind of-

2923
01:33:49.765 --> 01:33:51.129
Sorry, I'm geeking out too much on ultras.

2924
01:33:51.129 --> 01:33:52.610
No, and it's like, I love it,

2925
01:33:52.610 --> 01:33:56.090
but like I'm interested in the overlap

2926
01:33:56.090 --> 01:33:58.010
between what you do professionally,

2927
01:33:58.010 --> 01:34:00.790
and how your running, kind of informs that.

2928
01:34:00.790 --> 01:34:03.500
Like, how does it enhance your creativity?

2929
01:34:03.500 --> 01:34:05.060
What is the practice around that?

2930
01:34:05.060 --> 01:34:08.190
What are the kind of mindset tools that you develop,

2931
01:34:08.190 --> 01:34:11.450
that are applicable in both of these disciplines?

2932
01:34:11.450 --> 01:34:12.470
I've tried to figure out,

2933
01:34:12.470 --> 01:34:14.750
and it's hard to articulate some of it,

2934
01:34:14.750 --> 01:34:15.740
because everybody knows

2935
01:34:15.740 --> 01:34:17.610
that if they find something that's a practice,

2936
01:34:17.610 --> 01:34:19.210
like you just called it, whether it's yoga,

2937
01:34:19.210 --> 01:34:20.770
whether it's, you know, meditation,

2938
01:34:20.770 --> 01:34:23.720
whether it's running, is your brain gets into a zone,

2939
01:34:23.720 --> 01:34:25.700
that I think we don't have enough of nowadays,

2940
01:34:25.700 --> 01:34:28.180
which is you tune out, which is you're not thinking

2941
01:34:28.180 --> 01:34:29.760
of all the parts of your life that you have to do,

2942
01:34:29.760 --> 01:34:31.390
and you're not on your phone, or electronics,

2943
01:34:31.390 --> 01:34:32.840
or things that suck you in,

2944
01:34:32.840 --> 01:34:35.200
and that's when my biggest creative bursts happen.

2945
01:34:35.200 --> 01:34:37.630
They happen when I have to do something like you just said,

2946
01:34:37.630 --> 01:34:40.060
necessity is the mother of invention, constraints,

2947
01:34:40.060 --> 01:34:41.770
you have to have those.

2948
01:34:41.770 --> 01:34:46.200
And also I'm running, that's when I zone out.

2949
01:34:46.200 --> 01:34:47.720
There's something about moving,

2950
01:34:47.720 --> 01:34:49.980
and it's different for all different people.

2951
01:34:49.980 --> 01:34:51.680
It's funny because I don't think of running

2952
01:34:51.680 --> 01:34:53.780
as something I would enjoy, when I see someone running,

2953
01:34:53.780 --> 01:34:55.230
it doesn't look fun to me.

2954
01:34:55.230 --> 01:34:57.130
And when I was a runner in high school,

2955
01:34:57.130 --> 01:34:59.320
and I hated it so much, I still am that person,

2956
01:34:59.320 --> 01:35:02.505
but something about it now just evens me out.

2957
01:35:02.505 --> 01:35:05.510
The way my mind goes into that zone,

2958
01:35:05.510 --> 01:35:07.680
it's made me a better mentalist,

2959
01:35:07.680 --> 01:35:08.970
because I go into that same thing

2960
01:35:08.970 --> 01:35:10.997
where I can hyper focus in a way.

2961
01:35:10.997 --> 01:35:13.090
And it's kind of like visualizing a race,

2962
01:35:13.090 --> 01:35:14.270
before I'm ever at a race,

2963
01:35:14.270 --> 01:35:16.690
I've already run that race 100 times in my head.

2964
01:35:16.690 --> 01:35:18.460
I've already thought through where it's going,

2965
01:35:18.460 --> 01:35:21.480
and that same kind of methodical,

2966
01:35:21.480 --> 01:35:24.170
deciphering all the little angles of it,

2967
01:35:24.170 --> 01:35:26.880
while also just letting yourself go,

2968
01:35:26.880 --> 01:35:27.960
it's a little bit of both.

2969
01:35:27.960 --> 01:35:31.677
Like, mentalism, what I do, it's an art and a science,

2970
01:35:31.677 --> 01:35:34.240
and running I think is the same way.

2971
01:35:34.240 --> 01:35:37.840
Yeah, I mean, the way I think about it is

2972
01:35:37.840 --> 01:35:40.834
you're basically flexing this muscle

2973
01:35:40.834 --> 01:35:43.270
of being comfortable with being uncomfortable.

2974
01:35:43.270 --> 01:35:45.130
Like you're acclimating yourself

2975
01:35:45.130 --> 01:35:47.350
to a certain kind of discipline,

2976
01:35:47.350 --> 01:35:50.500
that just inures you to this idea

2977
01:35:50.500 --> 01:35:52.440
that you always have to be like iterating,

2978
01:35:52.440 --> 01:35:54.368
and creating, and pushing forward

2979
01:35:54.368 --> 01:35:56.310
because the running, you know,

2980
01:35:56.310 --> 01:35:58.580
is always like holding you to a account, right?

2981
01:35:58.580 --> 01:36:01.620
So if you carry that level

2982
01:36:01.620 --> 01:36:03.580
of discipline into your professional life,

2983
01:36:03.580 --> 01:36:05.630
like these two things can feed each other.

2984
01:36:05.630 --> 01:36:06.677
But I do think there's something

2985
01:36:06.677 --> 01:36:08.140
you kind of just briefly mentioned,

2986
01:36:08.140 --> 01:36:10.933
about not getting ahead of yourself.

2987
01:36:11.780 --> 01:36:14.370
In ultra running, you have to be present

2988
01:36:14.370 --> 01:36:15.750
with that pain and suffering.

2989
01:36:15.750 --> 01:36:17.950
If you have such a long distance to go,

2990
01:36:17.950 --> 01:36:19.310
and you're having a hard time,

2991
01:36:19.310 --> 01:36:21.270
and you start thinking about that,

2992
01:36:21.270 --> 01:36:23.040
you're toast, right?
Oh you're toast.

2993
01:36:23.040 --> 01:36:24.870
You have to be rooted in the moment.

2994
01:36:24.870 --> 01:36:27.620
And I would imagine not being a mentalist,

2995
01:36:27.620 --> 01:36:30.200
but thinking like for you to do what you do on stage,

2996
01:36:30.200 --> 01:36:32.460
like you have to be so in it,

2997
01:36:32.460 --> 01:36:34.100
in the moment of what you're doing,

2998
01:36:34.100 --> 01:36:36.170
in order to be able to execute.

2999
01:36:36.170 --> 01:36:37.670
Yeah, I never thought of it that way,

3000
01:36:37.670 --> 01:36:39.844
but you're absolutely right.

3001
01:36:39.844 --> 01:36:42.540
If I lose track of it, it's kinda like,

3002
01:36:42.540 --> 01:36:43.860
same thing we were saying, if you're thinking

3003
01:36:43.860 --> 01:36:45.530
about what you're gonna do after you catch the ball,

3004
01:36:45.530 --> 01:36:46.420
you don't catch the ball.

3005
01:36:46.420 --> 01:36:48.000
And a lot of my mess up,

3006
01:36:48.000 --> 01:36:49.473
have been that I'm thinking too far ahead,

3007
01:36:49.473 --> 01:36:50.950
and I really have to focus

3008
01:36:50.950 --> 01:36:52.110
on what's happening at that moment.

3009
01:36:52.110 --> 01:36:54.790
And running is, I don't know, it's a great escape,

3010
01:36:54.790 --> 01:36:58.300
it's so fun to not know what's gonna happen.

3011
01:36:58.300 --> 01:37:00.240
And with the ultras, the big challenge of it

3012
01:37:00.240 --> 01:37:03.030
is just knowing that you're going to suffer,

3013
01:37:03.030 --> 01:37:05.280
and seeing how you're gonna do in the suffering.

3014
01:37:05.280 --> 01:37:06.720
Yeah, and the longer it is,

3015
01:37:06.720 --> 01:37:07.963
the more of a mental thing it is.

3016
01:37:07.963 --> 01:37:10.852
Yeah, it's all mental.
And the mental

3017
01:37:10.852 --> 01:37:12.690
is what you do?
Right.

3018
01:37:12.690 --> 01:37:14.140
The mental is all I do.

3019
01:37:14.140 --> 01:37:15.880
Right.
They go hand in hand.

3020
01:37:15.880 --> 01:37:17.560
Yeah. Yeah.
I'm planning

3021
01:37:17.560 --> 01:37:18.800
to do a long run this summer,

3022
01:37:18.800 --> 01:37:20.170
I've been wanting to do it for years,

3023
01:37:20.170 --> 01:37:21.003
is I'm gonna run...

3024
01:37:21.003 --> 01:37:22.200
Because I'm out in Long Island,

3025
01:37:22.200 --> 01:37:24.610
some of the summer from (indistinct) to Manhattan,

3026
01:37:24.610 --> 01:37:27.256
which is around the length of a Spartathlon,

3027
01:37:27.256 --> 01:37:29.342
140 to 150, depending-
Oh, its that far, wow.

3028
01:37:29.342 --> 01:37:32.400
Well, it depends, as the Crow flies, it's a little less,

3029
01:37:32.400 --> 01:37:34.080
but, do you know what I mean?

3030
01:37:34.080 --> 01:37:35.439
You gotta kind of weave a little bit,

3031
01:37:35.439 --> 01:37:36.730
